(LST-34 — HISTORICAL, 0.4.14 and earlier. LANGUAGE.md:2437-2441 (0.4.14
 numbering) warned that `element N of` yields a REFERENCE to the child
 list, not a copy, so a child extracted before the parent reallocates
 "may point at freed memory". Probed at 5000 appends (far past any
 plausible growth threshold): the child stayed intact and readable. The
 hazard was hedged ("may"), so this was not a discrepancy in itself — but
 it was the one place in this section where the manual pointed at a
 use-after-free, and no leaf went near it.

 WITHDRAWN as of 0.4.15 (#111): the two-sentence limitation this probe
 tests is deleted from LANGUAGE.md outright, and the replacement text
 (2496-2515) asserts the opposite — `element N of` now copies, so there
 is no reference to dangle in the first place. This probe still compiles
 and its numbers still hold under 0.4.15 (a copy that stays intact is a
 weaker, always-true fact), but it no longer puts the WARNED-ABOUT claim
 on trial, because that claim no longer exists. Retained as the
 historical record. See `nested-elementcopy.vox` in the step-04 probe set
 for the direct copy-semantics probe that replaces this row's purpose.
